# FluidHelper Call adjacency

First, note this Fabric-side entry point to make it easier to trace back by type name. The file is at `Fabric/src/main/java/mezz/jei/fabric/platform/FluidHelper.java`; the repository index places it in the existing java module. The purpose at this stage is to read the code and check historical behavior.

The declaration visible at the top of the file is:

```java
package mezz.jei.fabric.platform;

import com.mojang.serialization.Codec;
import mezz.jei.api.fabric.constants.FabricTypes;
import mezz.jei.api.fabric.ingredients.fluids.IJeiFluidIngredient;
```

So far, there are only three direct neighbors here: `Codec`, `FabricTypes`, and `IJeiFluidIngredient`. The latter two names bring platform constants and the fluid ingredient type into this file; this page does not trace their upstream registration process any further. Whether `Codec` is used only for the codec fields in the declaration must be determined from the class's methods and fields; the file header alone does not establish that.

When revisiting this, first check the methods exposed by `FluidHelper` itself, then follow the usages of `IJeiFluidIngredient` to confirm the actual callers. If you only find type references and no runtime exit point, leave it as type adjacency; don't treat FabricTypes as the end of the call chain.

# FabricTypes Adjacent Calls

`FabricApi/src/main/java/mezz/jei/api/fabric/constants/FabricTypes.java` was looked for among the Fabric types. The first draft started by noting this section:

```java
package mezz.jei.api.fabric.constants;

import mezz.jei.api.fabric.ingredients.fluids.IJeiFluidIngredient;
import mezz.jei.api.fabric.ingredients.fluids.JeiFluidIngredient;
import mezz.jei.api.ingredients.IIngredientType;
```

At the time, this was interpreted as a fluid-type chain based on the names: `IJeiFluidIngredient` came first, `JeiFluidIngredient` appeared next, and `IIngredientType` came last. To find the Fabric fluid-related areas, open `FabricTypes.java` first, then follow these three names.

The old note treated names containing `I` as the interface side and `JeiFluidIngredient` as the concrete location on the Fabric side; `IIngredientType` was recorded as the type consolidation point. This sequence continued to be called "adjacent calls" afterward.

